---
import MainLayout from '../layouts/MainLayout.astro';
import BuyerDecisionSection from "../components/BuyerDecisionSection.astro";
import ServiceEvidencePanel from '../components/ServiceEvidencePanel.astro';
import serviceEvidence from '../data/serviceEvidence.json';
import LegacyHeader from '../components/LegacyHeader.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import ContactSection from '../components/ContactSection.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import ButtonPrimary from '../components/ButtonPrimary.astro';
import ButtonSecondary from '../components/ButtonSecondary.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'How is ecommerce SEO different from normal SEO?',
    answer: "On a brochure site you fix pages one at a time. On a store, one template decision repeats across every category or product that uses it, so a single fault in filters, variants, out-of-stock handling or structured data can affect thousands of URLs at once. We therefore check templates first, tie that work to the ranges you most want to sell, and report search traffic separately from recorded orders and revenue."
  },
  {
    question: 'Which ecommerce platforms do you work with?',
    answer: "Our store work is on Shopify, WooCommerce and WordPress. A store on Magento, BigCommerce or a custom or headless build is one we would assess first: we read what the platform outputs and tell you honestly whether the work suits us before any scope is agreed. On every platform, what we can implement ourselves depends on the theme, the installed apps and the developer access you give us. Before agreeing delivery we tell you which changes can be made in the CMS and which need engineering work, so nothing in the proposal relies on access we do not have."
  },
  {
    question: 'What results have you achieved for online stores?',
    answer: "Five stores have published Search Console figures. Emily Bridalwear rose from 7.41k to 17.6k organic clicks and Lost Dutchman Leather from 15.2k to 22.7k, each over three months against the same period a year earlier. Over six-month comparisons, Speediance Australia rose from 6,550 to 10,900 clicks, Speediance New Zealand from 927 to 2,570 and a boiler and heating spares retailer from 14.1k to 16.6k. Read each linked case for its period and limits. Search Console clicks are not orders or revenue, and one campaign does not set the outcome for another."
  },
  {
    question: 'How long does ecommerce SEO take to show results?',
    answer: "It depends on competition, access and the state of the store when we start. Template and indexation fixes reach many catalogue URLs at once, which is why they come first, but we do not promise a growth figure or a ranking deadline. We agree the scope and the reporting windows before work starts, then compare completed work with search evidence over matching periods, as the published cases do."
  },
  {
    question: 'Do you optimise product pages, category pages or both?',
    answer: "Both, in a deliberate order. We map searches to the categories and products you already have, then set priorities by demand, stock and commercial importance. Category work covers selection and comparison: the range, its filters and the links into it. Product work covers the item itself: variants, specifications, delivery and returns."
  },
  {
    question: 'Can you fix our store\'s technical SEO problems?',
    answer: "Yes, and technical work is usually where an ecommerce campaign starts. The faults we look for first are index bloat from filtered and tagged URLs, duplicate content across variants, thin or empty categories, broken canonical logic, slow templates hurting Core Web Vitals, missing or invalid product schema, and internal links that bury commercial pages. Our technical SEO service covers the diagnosis and the fixes, with each change checked on the live store after release."
  },
  {
    question: 'Does ecommerce SEO still matter with AI search and ChatGPT?',
    answer: "Yes. An assistant answering a shopping question draws on the same things a careful buyer checks: accurate specifications, stock, delivery and returns. We review those details on the store and can monitor a defined sample of AI shopping questions. Identifiable AI referrals are reported separately from completed orders, because a product mention alone is not a sale. Our published reviews of AI referral reports for Emily Bridalwear and Lost Dutchman Leather show how we read that data."
  },
  {
    question: 'How much does ecommerce SEO cost?',
    answer: "We publish no rate card, because catalogue size is only part of the scope. Template problems, content needs, target markets and your developers' capacity all change the work. You receive a written proposal that separates diagnosis, implementation, content and reporting, so you can see what the fee covers before deciding."
  },
  {
    question: 'Do you write product and category descriptions?',
    answer: "Yes, where it pays. We scope category copy, product descriptions and buying guides against your actual ranges and product information, starting with the templates and the lines that matter most commercially. Content is edited for the target audience in the Philippines, Australia, UK or USA, using the prices, units and buying conditions your team approves."
  },
  {
    question: 'Can you help with international or multi-store ecommerce SEO?',
    answer: "Yes. We review which products can be sold and delivered in each market, then check regional URLs, currencies, content and hreflang where appropriate. Each storefront is measured on its own. The linked Speediance projects cover separate storefronts; they are not proof that every market will grow together or that the sites never compete for a query."
  },
  {
    question: 'Do you build ecommerce stores, or only do SEO on them?',
    answer: "Both, as separate pieces of work. Ecommerce SEO improves the store you already have and does not assume you need a rebuild. If the store itself is the constraint, our development team scopes ecommerce web design from the catalogue outwards: product variants, category navigation, stock rules, delivery information and checkout. Integrations, payment configuration and catalogue migration are written into that scope, and for an existing store we map the important URLs and redirects before the structure changes."
  },
  {
    question: 'Will you work with our existing developers?',
    answer: "Yes. We agree who implements each change and give your developers the affected URLs or templates, the priority and the acceptance checks for each item. Whether we implement directly depends on the platform and access. Either way, changes are approved before release and checked on the live store afterwards."
  }
];
---

<MainLayout
  title="Ecommerce SEO Services for Online Stores | Bright Forge"
  description="Ecommerce SEO for Shopify and WooCommerce stores: category pages, product pages and template fixes, with published Search Console results for five shops."
  canonical="https://brightforge.com.ph/ecommerce-seo-services/"
  type="service"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
      itemListElement: [
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://brightforge.com.ph/' },
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Ecommerce SEO Services', item: 'https://brightforge.com.ph/ecommerce-seo-services/' }
      ]
    })} />
  </Fragment>

  <LegacyHeader slot="header" />

  <Hero
    badgeIcon="fas fa-shopping-cart"
    badgeText="Ecommerce SEO Specialists"
    title="Ecommerce SEO Services for <span>Categories, Products and Store Templates</span>"
    breadcrumbCurrent="Ecommerce SEO Services"
  >
    <Fragment slot="story">
      <p>Most of an online shop's search traffic should land on category and product pages. On many stores it cannot: the range has no category page worth ranking, filters spin out near-identical URLs by the thousand, and the products that sell best are buried deep in the navigation.</p>
      <p>The evidence is published. <a href="/case-studies/bridal-dress-designer-seo/">Emily Bridalwear</a> rose from 7.41k to 17.6k organic clicks over three months against the same period a year earlier, and the <a href="/case-studies/speediance-nz-fitness-seo/">Speediance New Zealand storefront</a> from 927 to 2,570 over six months. Those are Search Console clicks, not orders. We also set out the scope of our <a href="/case-studies/speediance-uk-fitness-seo/">UK fitness ecommerce work</a>, without figures.</p>
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="actions">
      <ButtonPrimary href="/contact/" text="Get an Ecommerce SEO Proposal" />
      <ButtonSecondary href="#store-results" text="See Store Results" />
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="visual">
      <div class="market-visual" aria-label="Published organic click comparisons for three online stores">
        <div class="market-node">
          <span>Emily Bridalwear</span>
          <strong>7.41k to 17.6k clicks, three months year on year</strong>
        </div>
        <div class="market-line"></div>
        <div class="market-node forge">
          <span>Lost Dutchman Leather</span>
          <strong>15.2k to 22.7k clicks, three months year on year</strong>
        </div>
        <div class="market-line"></div>
        <div class="market-node">
          <span>Speediance NZ</span>
          <strong>927 to 2,570 site-wide clicks, six-month comparison</strong>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
  </Hero>

  <section class="proof-strip" aria-label="Ecommerce SEO proof points">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="proof-grid">
        <div><strong>Five stores with published figures</strong><span>Search Console comparisons, periods stated</span></div>
        <div><strong>Regional storefronts</strong><span>Speediance Australia and New Zealand, measured separately</span></div>
        <div><strong>AI referral reviews</strong><span>Emily Bridalwear and Lost Dutchman Leather</span></div>
        <div><strong>Platform-specific review</strong><span>Shopify, WooCommerce and WordPress</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section dark" id="who-for">
    <div class="container two-col">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Who this is for</p>
        <h2>An ecommerce SEO agency for stores that <span>compete on more than ad spend</span></h2>
        <p>We fix that where it pays, at the template. We review categories, filters, variants, internal links and product information against search demand, then write a proposal that separates template changes from content work and names what needs your developer or your merchandising team.</p>
        <p class="lead">You stock products people search for, yet the range has no category page that could rank for it. Or your filters create duplicate URLs while the products you most want to sell stay hidden. Paid traffic covers the gap for as long as you keep paying. We find the page and template changes that close it.</p>
        <p>We work with stores that want category terms, product long-tail searches and buying-intent content pulling in the same direction, whether the competition is a marketplace, a big-box retailer or a well-funded DTC rival.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card-grid small">
        <article class="card"><h3>DTC brands</h3><p>Category selection, product information and buying guides for shoppers weighing your range against marketplace alternatives. Lost Dutchman Leather, an Arizona-based wallet brand, is our published example.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Multi-country stores</h3><p>Regional page targeting based on actual stock, delivery coverage, currency and search demand, with each market measured separately, as the Speediance Australia and New Zealand storefronts are.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Technical catalogues</h3><p>Spares, parts and specification-heavy stores, where buyers search by model number and compatibility and an accurate page wins the order.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Replatforming stores</h3><p>Moving platforms? Our <a href="/website-migration-services/">migration service</a> plans the redirects, checks the new templates and monitors the move, so the rankings you have earned travel with you.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section darker" id="store-results">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head centered">
        <p class="eyebrow">Published store results</p>
        <h2>Five stores, <span>each with its Search Console figures</span></h2>
        <p>Every card states the period and what the comparison cannot show. Four are click comparisons for named clients, one is an unnamed parts retailer, and the last is a review of an AI referral report. Click totals are not orders or revenue.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="results-grid">
        <article class="result-card">
          <p class="result-tag">Bridal Fashion · UK</p>
          <h3>Emily Bridalwear: +137.5% clicks, three-month year-on-year comparison</h3>
          <p>Search Console shows 17.6k clicks against 7.41k for the last three months versus the same period a year earlier, with impressions up from 342k to 746k and average position from 24.8 to 14.9. It records search performance, not the cause of the change or the number of dresses sold.</p>
          <a href="/case-studies/bridal-dress-designer-seo/">Read the case study →</a>
        </article>
        <article class="result-card">
          <p class="result-tag">Leather Goods · DTC</p>
          <h3>Lost Dutchman Leather: +49.3% clicks, CTR 3.1% → 4.5%</h3>
          <p>Clicks rose from 15.2k to 22.7k on almost the same impressions (485k to 500k), because click-through rate climbed from 3.1% to 4.5%. The comparison is the last three months against the same period a year earlier. These are period totals, not a monthly uplift.</p>
          <a href="/case-studies/leather-wallet-ecommerce-seo/">Read the case study →</a>
        </article>
        <article class="result-card">
          <p class="result-tag">Fitness Equipment · AU + NZ</p>
          <h3>Speediance: +66% and +177% clicks, six-month comparisons</h3>
          <p>The Australian storefront rose from 6,550 to 10,900 clicks even though its average position slipped from 24.9 to 28.1. The New Zealand storefront rose from 927 to 2,570. Both are separate site-wide comparisons. They are not country-filtered and do not prove the storefronts never compete for the same search.</p>
          <a href="/case-studies/speediance-au-fitness-seo/">Read the case study →</a>
        </article>
        <article class="result-card">
          <p class="result-tag">Technical Parts Ecommerce</p>
          <h3>Heating spares retailer: +18% clicks on a 1M+ impression store</h3>
          <p>Clicks rose from 14.1k to 16.6k and impressions from 1.06M to 1.17M across successive six-month windows. The catalogue is specification-heavy, where model numbers and compatibility decide the click. The figures are period totals, not monthly ones.</p>
          <a href="/case-studies/boiler-heating-spares-seo/">Read the case study →</a>
        </article>
        <article class="result-card">
          <p class="result-tag">Fitness Equipment · UK</p>
          <h3>Speediance UK fitness ecommerce</h3>
          <p>Technical, product-intent and content work for a UK home-gym storefront. This one is a project review: it sets out the scope and the campaign approach and publishes no traffic figures.</p>
          <a href="/case-studies/speediance-uk-fitness-seo/">Read the case study →</a>
        </article>
        <article class="result-card">
          <p class="result-tag">Review focus · AI search</p>
          <h3>Bridal retail: support the appointment decision</h3>
          <p>Our review of Emily Bridalwear's AI referral report found category, product and fabric pages among the landing pages. Design ranges, sizing, stockists and appointment information answer different questions, so we check where each belongs and how a shopper moves from a dress to an enquiry.</p>
          <a href="/case-studies/ai-bridal-ecommerce-geo/">Related AI-search review →</a>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section dark" id="services">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head centered">
        <p class="eyebrow">What we deliver</p>
        <h2>Technical and content <span>SEO for online stores</span></h2>
        <p>An ecommerce campaign combines technical, content, authority and measurement work. We sequence it by what it could earn on your ranges, which rarely matches a fixed monthly task list.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="service-grid">
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-sitemap"></i><h3>Catalogue architecture</h3><p>Which categories should exist and what they are called, plus faceted navigation control, internal linking and pagination, so commercial pages can rank and shoppers can reach them.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-cogs"></i><h3>Technical SEO</h3><p>Index bloat, duplicate variants, canonical logic, Core Web Vitals and crawl efficiency across thousands of URLs. See our <a href="/technical-seo-services-philippines/">technical SEO service</a>.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-tags"></i><h3>Product page SEO</h3><p>Titles, descriptions, specifications, product schema and images checked against the actual item and its variants, with review markup where eligible. We do not mark up what a shopper cannot verify on the page.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-pen-nib"></i><h3>Commerce content</h3><p>Category copy, buying guides and comparison content mapped to real search demand via our <a href="/content-seo-services-philippines/">content SEO service</a>. Every guide links to the categories it discusses.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-key"></i><h3>Keyword &amp; demand mapping</h3><p>Commercial head terms, long-tail product demand and research-stage queries, each assigned to the page type that should answer it, so two pages never chase the same search.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-link"></i><h3>Authority building</h3><p>Relevance-led <a href="/backlink-seo-services-philippines/">link building</a>: digital PR angles and industry placements, never bulk packages.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-robot"></i><h3>AI search optimisation</h3><p>Product specifications, availability, shipping and returns information that buyers and assistants can check. We separate observed AI citations from referral visits and orders. See <a href="/ai-search-optimization-services-philippines/">AI search optimisation</a>.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-chart-line"></i><h3>Revenue reporting</h3><p>Monthly reports tied to GA4 ecommerce data: rankings, clicks, revenue movement, the work completed and the next priorities, with search clicks kept distinct from recorded orders.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <BuyerDecisionSection id="store-page-roles" eyebrow="Give each page a commercial job" title="The homepage cannot target" emphasis="the whole catalogue.">
    <p>Ecommerce homepage SEO has three jobs: say what the store sells, establish trust and send shoppers towards the main ranges. Ask the homepage to rank for every product category as well and it does all three badly.</p>
    <dl>
      <dt>Homepage: brand and range</dt><dd>Show the core offer, the buying conditions and clear paths into priority categories. Internal links should help a shopper choose a range. They are not a place to repeat every keyword the business wants to rank for.</dd>
      <dt>Categories: selection and comparison</dt><dd>Give each commercially distinct range one clear owner. Review filters, product availability and supporting information so the page can answer a category search without creating an indexable URL for every possible combination. Our article on <a href="/blog/ecommerce-category-pages-that-can-rank/">category pages that can rank</a> goes into the detail.</dd>
      <dt>Products: the purchase decision</dt><dd>Match the page to the actual item, variant and specification. Decide what happens to out-of-stock and discontinued products, preserve useful URLs and avoid repeating category copy where a buyer needs product detail.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>We map those roles before changing a single title or expanding any content. When the constraint is the store itself and search visibility is not the problem, <a href="/development/#store-build-scope">review the ecommerce development scope</a> before committing to more SEO activity.</p>
  </BuyerDecisionSection>

  <section class="section darker" id="platforms">
    <div class="container two-col">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Platforms</p>
        <h2>Check what the platform outputs <span>before blaming the platform</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">Stores are often told the platform is the problem. Usually it is a setting, a theme or an app. Our first technical review reads what your platform actually outputs: the links, canonicals, structured data and HTML that shoppers and crawlers receive.</p>
        <p>Shopify SEO includes checking the duplicate product paths created through collections. WooCommerce SEO includes reviewing the filters and plugins that can create competing archives. Those are the platforms our store work is on. Layered navigation on Magento and rendering on a headless build raise different questions, and we would assess such a store before agreeing to take it on. Either way, we confirm the real fault before asking a developer to change a template.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="timezone-card">
        <h3>Platform coverage</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Shopify &amp; Shopify Plus</strong>: collection architecture, tag duplication, app bloat control</li>
          <li><strong>WooCommerce</strong>: crawl budget, filtered URLs, performance on WordPress hosting</li>
          <li><strong>WordPress content</strong>: editorial pages that support the catalogue, with headings, canonicals and structured data kept consistent after template changes</li>
          <li><strong>Magento, BigCommerce &amp; headless</strong>: assessed first. We review the output and confirm the fit before agreeing any scope</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="pf-section" id="seo-or-rebuild">
    <div class="pf-wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">SEO or a new store</p>
      <h2>Do you need <span>ecommerce SEO or a rebuild</span>?</h2>
      <p class="pf-lede">We do both, and we keep them apart. Most stores that come to us need better pages on the platform they already have. A few are held back by the store itself.</p>
      <div class="pf-table-wrap"><table class="pf-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">Ecommerce SEO on your current store</th><th scope="col">A store build or rebuild</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">When it fits</th><td>The store works and takes orders, but categories and products are hard to find in search.</td><td>The catalogue, templates or checkout cannot do what the business needs, whatever the SEO.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">What we work on</th><td>Category structure, filters and duplicates, product pages, internal links, content and reporting.</td><td>Product variants, category navigation, stock rules, delivery information and checkout, established before a design or platform is recommended.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">What is written into the scope</th><td>Which changes we make in the CMS and which need your developer, with acceptance checks for each.</td><td>Integrations, payment configuration and catalogue migration. Nobody should have to assume they are included.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">What protects your rankings</th><td>Changes are approved before release and checked on the live store afterwards.</td><td>Important URLs, redirects and category relationships are mapped before the structure changes.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p class="pf-note">The build column summarises our <a href="/development/#store-build-scope">ecommerce development scope</a>. A platform move is planned through our <a href="/website-migration-services/">migration service</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section dark" id="process">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head centered">
        <p class="eyebrow">How a campaign runs</p>
        <h2>Four phases of <span>an ecommerce SEO campaign</span></h2>
      </div>

      <div class="timeline">
        <article><span>Diagnose</span><h3>Store diagnosis</h3><p>A full crawl, an indexation audit, a GA4 and Search Console review by page type and a competitor gap analysis. The output is a priority list mapped to the ranges that earn your revenue.</p></article>
        <article><span>Implement</span><h3>Template-level fixes</h3><p>Prioritised changes to canonicals, structured data, internal links and category templates, tested on a sample before wider rollout and checked on the live store afterwards.</p></article>
        <article><span>Publish</span><h3>Content and authority</h3><p>Category copy for the highest-value ranges first, then products, buying guides and link acquisition for the terms your revenue depends on.</p></article>
        <article><span>Review</span><h3>Review and prioritise</h3><p>Compare the released changes with search data and recorded orders over matching periods, then choose the next priorities with your merchandising team.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section faq-section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <div class="section-head centered">
        <p class="eyebrow">Ecommerce SEO FAQs</p>
        <h2><span>Questions store owners ask</span> before working with us</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-list">
        {faqs.map((faq) => (
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>{faq.question}</summary>
            <p>{faq.answer}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta-section">
    <div class="container narrow cta-box">
      <p class="eyebrow">Ready to talk?</p>
      <h2>Get a straight answer about <span>SEO for your store</span></h2>
      <p>Send us your platform, the ranges you most want to sell, your target markets and how much developer time you have. We will tell you whether the first piece of work should be a catalogue review, a template repair or a content brief, and we usually reply within one business day.</p>
      <div class="cta-actions">
        <a href="/contact/" class="cta-primary">Get an Ecommerce SEO Proposal</a>
        <a href="/case-studies/" class="cta-secondary">View All Case Studies</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <ServiceEvidencePanel panel={serviceEvidence['ecommerce-seo-services']} />

  <ContactSection slot="contact" />
  <Footer slot="footer" />
</MainLayout>

<style>
.container {
  width: min(1180px, calc(100% - 2rem));
  margin: 0 auto;
}

.narrow {
  width: min(900px, calc(100% - 2rem));
}

.market-visual {
  position: relative;
  display: grid;
  gap: 1.1rem;
  max-width: 480px;
  margin: 0 auto;
  padding: 1.5rem;
  border: 1px solid rgba(255, 107, 53, 0.25);
  border-radius: 28px;
  background: linear-gradient(145deg, rgba(25, 33, 46, 0.09), rgba(25, 33, 46, 0.09));
  box-shadow: 0 25px 80px rgba(25, 33, 46, 0.09);
}

.market-node {
  padding: 1.2rem;
  border-radius: 18px;
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06);
  border: 1px solid rgba(25, 33, 46, 0.09);
}

.market-node.forge {
  border-color: rgba(255, 107, 53, 0.35);
}

.market-node span {
  display: block;
  color: #b83e12;
  font-size: 0.82rem;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
}

.market-node strong {
  display: block;
  color: #19212e;
  font-size: 1.05rem;
}

.market-line {
  height: 26px;
  width: 2px;
  background: linear-gradient(#ff6b35, transparent);
  margin-left: 1.2rem;
}

.proof-strip {
  background: #ffffff;
  border-top: 1px solid rgba(25, 33, 46, 0.08);
  border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(25, 33, 46, 0.08);
  padding: 1.5rem 0;
}

.proof-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
  gap: 1rem;
}

.proof-grid div {
  padding: 1.1rem;
  border-radius: 16px;
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.045);
  border: 1px solid rgba(25, 33, 46, 0.08);
}

.proof-grid strong,
.proof-grid span {
  display: block;
}

.proof-grid strong {
  color: #19212e;
  margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
}

.proof-grid span {
  color: #465365;
  font-size: 0.92rem;
}

.section {
  padding: 5.5rem 0;
}

.dark {
  background: #f7f8fa;
}

.darker {
  background: #ffffff;
}

.two-col {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.9fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr);
  gap: 3rem;
  align-items: center;
}

.eyebrow {
  color: #b83e12;
